  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The Nino 3.4 amplitude is defined from anomalies relative to a stated 1950–2014 monthly climatology, and this fragment fixes how anomaly baselines and calendar-month statistics must be interpreted.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build each model's index with `compute_enso_nino_index` on its own output and do not pass `climatology='woa23'`: the anomaly must come from the field's own 1950-2014 monthly climatology, else the model's mean-state bias enters the index and it is no longer an ENSO index.",
        "Match each month against the climatology of its own calendar month (this removes the seasonal cycle) and state the climatology and baseline used — the tools record both in the result's attrs; the exceedance metric here counts months with |anomaly| > 1.0, so persistence (`min_duration`) plays no role."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "The Nino 3.4 SST anomaly is a regional surface mean, which this fragment says must be area-weighted over the top level using the grid-cell areas.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The Nino 3.4 average over 5S-5N, 170W-120W must be area-weighted on each source's grid with weights restricted to the same valid cells as the data (NaN-aware); an unweighted `mean()` over (y, x) is wrong on a curvilinear grid."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The models' `tos` is upper-boundary bulk temperature rather than skin SST, so comparing its Nino 3.4 amplitude with ERSSTv5 carries that definitional caveat.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`tos` is the bulk temperature of the top model cell, not a skin value, and is archived for these historical runs, so use it directly with the default `depth_level=None` — no first-level `thetao` substitute is needed.",
        "The model-minus-ERSSTv5 amplitude difference is therefore a bulk-top-cell-versus-observation comparison; do not describe `tos` as a skin temperature."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245.md",
      "sha256": "be5515ae7b8c8826c4e329bddcd5cbadc8e3e000292b7f6e83e1537af36a990c",
      "reason": "The historical runs are coupled with free internal variability and are not year-synchronized with observations, which matters when comparing 1950–2014 amplitudes to ERSSTv5.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Coupled historical runs have their own internal variability: compare only statistics (the index standard deviation, the seasonal-cycle statistics), never individual years or event timing — a model's peak month or El Nino year need not match the observed one.",
        "With a single member per model, differences in amplitude between models and ERSSTv5 cannot be split into forced versus internal-variability parts; state this caveat."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are on native `gn` grids while GFDL-ESM4 is `gr` regridded output, which affects the regional weighting and masks.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 is `gr`: its values are already remapped to a 1x1 degree grid with a regridded land–sea mask, so label its numbers as taken on that archive grid.",
        "Horizontal resolution differs about 2.5x between CESM2 (100 km) and MPI-ESM1-2-LR (250 km); features narrower than the coarse grid are not comparable in amplitude."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "This fragment warns that regridding changes land-sea masks and conservation, so the Nino 3.4 regional means should be formed on each source's available grid with its own cell areas.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Compute every number (regional index, its standard deviation, per-month standard deviations) on each source's own grid with its own cell areas; the two cell-by-cell comparisons here need no regridding, so no regrid method is quoted."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-static-grid-fields",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-static-grid-fields.md",
      "sha256": "2ad0548ca0993fa57d8f1fad27a267dcf9c0e1d007a74343d4bbdbf5b4f1ca17",
      "reason": "`areacello` is the tracer-cell horizontal area needed for the area-weighted Nino 3.4 regional mean, and this fragment defines it.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Use each model's published `areacello` as the area weight (it is the tracer-cell area, zero or missing over land) and report the origin recorded in `attrs['area_source']` when it feeds the regional average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "model-gfdl-esm4-reported-biases",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "datasets/model-gfdl-esm4-reported-biases.md",
      "sha256": "8ec79c87d10734218374fc56bca956ea159125aeea65b344b1476fb09481f1fe",
      "reason": "GFDL-ESM4 is reported to have a slightly strong ~4-year ENSO, background relevant to interpreting its simulated Nino 3.4 amplitude.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4's ENSO is reported slightly stronger than observed with a longer period (~4 years against ~3.3), so a positive amplitude bias for GFDL-ESM4 is consistent with its documentation; these are context, not target values."
      ]
